"A friend says, \"When I hold my arms out, my base of support gets bigger.\" Is your friend right? What do the arms really do?": "385a136fa6b5",
"A wide, low stance is more stable than a tall, narrow one.": "509fbac12431",
"Balance is not something you have. It is something you do. Your eyes, the balance organs in your inner ears, and sensors in your muscles and joints keep telling your brain where your body is, and your muscles keep making small corrections. Even when you stand quietly, you sway a little the whole time. Stand on one foot and close your eyes, and you will feel how much your eyes were helping.": "fad98a01af47",
"Bend your knees.": "861a340b15fe",
"Bending your knees lowers your center of gravity.": "eb6b272f1c19",
"Do not lean too far over the railing.": "f11ebf840915",
"Every part of you has weight: your head, your arms, your legs. But you can think of all that weight as pulling down at one single point. That point is your center of gravity. When an adult stands up straight, it is inside the body, about 55 percent of the way up from the floor, near the height of the navel. It does not stay in one place. Raise your arms and it moves up. Bend your knees and it moves down.": "55041e2283b5",
"Find your own space.": "d748127c38d8",
"Here is the rule. Imagine a line that drops straight down from your center of gravity to the floor. As long as that line lands inside your base of support, you stay up. When it lands outside, you begin to tip, and you have to take a step, grab something, or fall. Try the 3D model above: the figure tips at the very moment the gold dot leaves the green shape.": "c1f35f96ba57",
"Hold it for ten seconds.": "426194595474",
"Hold your arms out to the sides.": "f3b573e6f2fd",
"Look straight ahead.": "afae588889dd",
"Now look at the floor. Your base of support is the area marked out by the parts of you that touch the ground. When you stand on two feet, it is more than two footprints. It also includes the floor between them. Stand on one foot, and the base shrinks to a single footprint. Rise onto your toes, and it shrinks again.": "ed9f4809487d",
"She lost her balance and had to take a step.": "ab1d6ed972c1",
"Spread your feet apart.": "701910ccdca6",
"Stand on one foot.": "841af68686ae",
"Stand up and put your feet together. Now lift one foot off the floor. You wobble, and your arms fly out to the sides. Nobody taught you to do that. Your body works all the time to keep you from falling, even when you think you are standing still. To see how it does this, you need two ideas: the center of gravity and the base of support.": "4844cb62c61a",
"Stand up and put your feet together. Now lift one foot off the floor. You wobble, and your arms fly out to the sides. Nobody taught you to do that. Your body works all the time to keep you from falling, even when you think you are standing still. To see how it does this, you need two ideas: the center of gravity and the base of support. Every part of you has weight: your head, your arms, your legs. But you can think of all that weight as pulling down at one single point. That point is your center of gravity. When an adult stands up straight, it is inside the body, about 55 percent of the way up from the floor, near the height of the navel. It does not stay in one place. Raise your arms and it moves up. Bend your knees and it moves down. Now look at the floor. Your base of support is the area marked out by the parts of you that touch the ground. When you stand on two feet, it is more than two footprints. It also includes the floor between them. Stand on one foot, and the base shrinks to a single footprint. Rise onto your toes, and it shrinks again. Here is the rule. Imagine a line that drops straight down from your center of gravity to the floor. As long as that line lands inside your base of support, you stay up. When it lands outside, you begin to tip, and you have to take a step, grab something, or fall. Try the 3D model above: the figure tips at the very moment the gold dot leaves the green shape. There are two ways to make yourself harder to tip. First, spread your feet. A bigger base gives that line more room to move before it reaches the edge. Second, bend your knees. A lower center of gravity has to swing farther before the line reaches the edge. Athletes do both. Watch a judo player or a basketball defender: feet wide, knees bent. But a base is only wide in the direction you spread your feet. Feet side by side resist a push from the side. One foot in front resists a push from the front. Balance is not something you have. It is something you do. Your eyes, the balance organs in your inner ears, and sensors in your muscles and joints keep telling your brain where your body is, and your muscles keep making small corrections. Even when you stand quietly, you sway a little the whole time. Stand on one foot and close your eyes, and you will feel how much your eyes were helping.": "c74fbf808c49",
